Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {Air pollution, increased hydrogeological risk, and the urban heat island
   effect are significant consequences of human overcrowding in urban
   areas. To mitigate these adverse impacts, urban greening has emerged as
   a viable solution. Green areas in cities offer a range of benefits,
   including easily measurable ones like oxygen production and pollutant
   reduction, as well as more intangible benefits related to the
   socio-cultural well-being of residents. This paper aims to deepen the
   understanding of these less quantifiable urban ecosystem services
   through the active engagement of citizens in Boadilla del Monte, a
   medium-sized city in the Autonomous Community of Madrid, Spain. An
   online survey was conducted to examine residents' relationships with
   urban greenery, focusing on their usage, perceptions, and awareness of
   the interconnections between climate change, ecosystem services, and
   green areas. The findings indicate that while citizens possess a solid
   understanding of environmental issues and the benefits provided by urban
   green areas, there is less familiarity with the term ``Ecosystem
   Services{''} and even less with the concept of assigning monetary value
   to these services. Regarding climate change, most respondents, aside
   from a small group of skeptics, acknowledge its local impact.
   Additionally, the study serves as a valuable tool for assessing citizen
   satisfaction with the Municipality's commitment to creating spaces for
   social integration, such as parks and urban gardens, while reinforcing
   strategies for adapting the city to ongoing climate change. By engaging
   citizens in this research, we hope to enhance public awareness and
   foster a community-oriented approach to sustainable urban planning.},
