   Stavros},
Title = {Fine-Scale Estimation of Urban Biogenic CO2 Fluxes: A Novel
   Framework Integrating Multiple Versions of Vegetation Photosynthesis and
   Respiration Models and In Situ Measurements},
Journal = {EARTHS FUTURE},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {14},
Number = {3},
Month = {FEB 25},
Abstract = {Estimating biogenic CO2 fluxes is essential for quantifying urban
   anthropogenic emissions, yet urban vegetation heterogeneity presents a
   significant challenge to making accurate estimations. We have developed
   a 10-meter spatial, hourly temporal resolution biogenic CO2 flux
   estimation framework based on the Vegetation Photosynthesis and
   Respiration Model (VPRM) and its variants (UrbanVPRM and VPRM-modified).
   Unlike lower-resolution models, our approach captures finer-scale
   variability, particularly in fragmented urban green spaces like street
   trees and lawns. Results show that vegetation in Munich offsets similar
   to 2\% of annual anthropogenic CO2 emissions in the study domain, with
   tree-covered areas as primary sinks and grasslands as net sources.
   During summer, daytime CO2 uptake can match or exceed anthropogenic
   emissions. Evaluations employing city park field measurements and eddy
   covariance towers confirm strong performance of our models while
   revealing strengths and limitations among the three versions of VPRM.
   These findings highlight the value of high-resolution modeling in
   improving urban carbon flux assessments.},
